{#- Crawlers read robots.txt ONLY at the root of an address (RFC 9309; Google: "crawlers don't
    check for robots.txt files in subdirectories"). This site is a GitHub Pages PROJECT site
    (https://neta65.github.io/aagrapevine/), so this file is published at
    /aagrapevine/robots.txt and no search engine ever reads it. The file that counts is
    https://neta65.github.io/robots.txt, and only the NETA65 account's own user-site
    repository, NETA65/neta65.github.io, can publish it: its ready-to-upload files are in
    docs/user-site/, the owner's steps in README → "Search engines", and the Weekly link check
    reads it every Sunday (scripts/ops/root_robots.py). Until that repository exists the root
    answers 404, which means "crawl everything": the site is crawled anyway, only the Sitemap
    line below goes unseen (so the sitemap is also submitted by hand in Google Search Console
    and Bing Webmaster Tools — same README section).
    This file becomes the live one only with a custom domain (README §12, "Using your own
    address"): then it IS the root file. So keep it as it is — everything allowed, no Disallow
    lines (Google needs the CSS, scripts and JSON files to show the pages), and the sitemap.
    The Sitemap line needs an absolute URL: site.url comes from config/site.yml (or the
    SITE_URL the GitHub Action sets). Without one it is left out. -#}
User-agent: *
